# Tech Stack & Best Practices

Guidance for projects built with **Nuxt 4 + Vue 3 + TypeScript + Vite**. This is framework-level convention, not project-specific.

## Tech Stack

- **Framework:** Nuxt 4 (built on Vue 3 + Vue Router + Vite)
- **UI:** Vue 3 (`<script setup>` SFCs, Composition API)
- **Language:** TypeScript (strict mode recommended)
- **Bundler:** Vite (via Nuxt's build layer — do not configure Vite separately unless overriding `nuxt.config.ts`)

## Best Practices

### Vue 3
- Use `<script setup lang="ts">` for all components.
- Prefer the Composition API; keep logic in composables under `composables/`.
- Use `ref`/`computed`/`watch` from `vue`; avoid the Options API.
- Keep components small, single-responsibility, and presentation-focused.

### TypeScript
- Enable `strict: true` in `tsconfig`.
- Type component props with `defineProps<{ ... }>()` and emits with `defineEmits<{ ... }>()`.
- Avoid `any`; use `unknown` and narrow when the type is truly dynamic.
- Let Nuxt's auto-generated types (`*.d.ts`) handle route/asset/runtime-config typing.

### Nuxt 4
- Use file-based routing under `pages/`; avoid manual router config.
- Place server logic under `server/` (Nitro): `server/api`, `server/utils`, `server/middleware`.
- Access runtime config via `useRuntimeConfig()` (server) and `useRuntimeConfig().public` (client).
- Use `useAsyncData`/`useFetch` for SSR-safe data fetching.
- Register modules via `nuxt.config.ts` `modules` array, not by manual plugin wiring.

### Vite
- Rely on Nuxt's Vite integration; add custom Vite options through `nuxt.config.ts` `vite` key if needed.
- Use `~` / `@` aliases provided by Nuxt instead of relative `../../../` imports.
- Keep build-time-only deps in `devDependencies`.

## Directory Conventions

Different kinds of projects follow different layouts. Pick the one that matches the package you are working in.

### Test code

- Place unit tests under `<pkg>/tests/unit/`, mirroring the `src/` layout, with files named `*.test.ts`.
  - Alternatively colocate as `src/**/*.test.ts` next to the code under test.
- Put shared fixtures and mocks under `<pkg>/tests/fixtures/`.
- Use **Vitest**; configure via `<pkg>/vitest.config.ts` (or the root config in a monorepo).
- Test files are excluded from the published/built output.

### Nuxt App

```
app/                  # Frontend application
├── app.vue           # Root component
├── pages/            # File-based routes (SSR + CSR)
├── components/       # Auto-imported Vue components
├── composables/      # Auto-imported composables
├── layouts/          # Layout templates
└── assets/           # Uncompiled assets (processed by Vite)
server/               # Nitro backend
├── api/              # API route handlers (auto-registered)
├── middleware/       # Server middleware
└── utils/            # Server-side utilities
public/               # Static assets served at root (untouched)
nuxt.config.ts        # Nuxt configuration entry
.env / .env.example   # Env files live inside the app, not the repo root
```

### Nuxt Module

```
src/
├── module.ts          # defineNuxtModule entry (Builder / setup hook)
│
├── builder/           # Build-time logic (runs only during `nuxt prepare`/`build`)
│   ├── scanner.ts     # Scan the filesystem / source tree for targets
│   ├── metadata.ts    # Collect & normalize discovered metadata
│   ├── manifest.ts    # Assemble the generated manifest (virtual module payload)
│   └── yaml.ts        # Parse / serialize YAML config (build-time only)
│
└── runtime/           # Files shipped to the Nuxt/Nitro runtime
    ├── plugins/       # Auto-registered Nuxt plugins (client + server)
    │   └── bootstrap.ts
    └── utils/         # Runtime helpers (safe to import from plugins)
package.json           # "type": "module"; exports map; peerDeps on nuxt/@nuxt/kit
tsconfig.json          # Extends the shared base; emits .d.ts
```

- The module entry (`src/module.ts`) runs at **build time**; it must not import runtime files directly. Runtime files are injected via `addPlugin` / `addServerPlugin` / virtual modules.
- Keep all build-time concerns (scanning, codegen, config parsing) isolated under `builder/`; the `builder/` directory must never be shipped to the runtime.
- Keep framework-agnostic logic out of the module; depend on a separate TS library when possible.
- `runtime/utils/` holds helpers that may be imported by `runtime/plugins/`; anything build-only stays in `builder/`.

### Generic TypeScript Library

```
src/
├── index.ts          # Public barrel re-exporting the public API
└── <feature>/        # Implementation modules
tests/unit/           # Unit tests (vitest), mirroring src/
package.json          # "type": "module"; "exports" + "types"; no Nuxt/Vue deps
tsconfig.json         # Extends the shared base; emits .d.ts
```

- Must stay framework-agnostic — never import `nuxt`, `@nuxt/kit`, or `nitropack`.
- Expose the public API only through `src/index.ts` (barrel).

### Composite Project (pnpm monorepo)

```
packages/             # Publishable libraries (TS libs, Nuxt modules)
apps/                 # Deployable applications (Nuxt apps, docs sites)
docker/               # Container / Docker assets (e.g. postgres)
scripts/              # Dev/build/release/CI-CD helper scripts
pnpm-workspace.yaml   # Globs: packages/*, apps/*
tsconfig.base.json    # Shared base tsconfig extended by every package
package.json          # Workspace root: cross-package scripts (build/typecheck/test)
```

- Reusable libraries go in `packages/*`; runnable apps go in `apps/*`.
- Internal cross-package deps use the `workspace:*` protocol.
- Shared TS config lives in `tsconfig.base.json`; each package extends it.
- Root pnpm scripts orchestrate cross-package tasks (`pnpm -r`, `--filter`).

### General conventions
- Follow Nuxt 4 directory conventions for apps; do not reinvent routing or module loading.
- Components in `components/` and composables in `composables/` are auto-imported — name them clearly and avoid collisions.
- Server API routes live in `server/api/` and are auto-registered by Nitro; shared helpers go in `server/utils/`.
- Keep `public/` for static files only; compiled assets belong in `app/assets/`.
- Configuration belongs in `nuxt.config.ts`; avoid hard-coding env values — use runtime config + `.env`.
- Tests mirror source layout and are never part of the published/built output.
